GEFS native suspension test
===========================

Build with NetBSD make and matching kernel headers, with the tested GEFS
module loaded. Use disposable mounted image files. The path must name an
existing regular file, not the mountpoint.

    make
    modload -s path=/mnt/gefs/file -i seconds=35 ./suspendtest.kmod
    modunload suspendtest
    modload -s path=/mnt/gefs/file -b inject=true ./suspendtest.kmod
    modunload suspendtest
    modload -s path=/mnt/gefs/file -s path2=/mnt/fork/file ./suspendtest.kmod
    modunload suspendtest

The module holds a durable suspension, performs waiting sync, and checks
that device reads, writes and cache flushes do not change during the hold.
Set vfs.gefs.interval and vfs.gefs.lazymax to 1 to cross background deadlines;
restore their previous values afterward. The injection case records EIO on
the vnode and checks suspension rollback, then restores the error field.
It does not inject a physical device failure.

For admission testing, start a 10-second hold in the background, then write
to a file on that mount. Verify the writer is blocked during the hold and
completes after resume. Also exercise normal unmount, a busy unmount followed
by a successful write, forced unmount, and a read-only mount updated to
read-write. These use the same NetBSD suspension protocol as s2idle.

The 2026-10-14 NetBSD 11 VM run passed all of the above, including two labels
sharing one vnd device and a 35-second hold spanning the syncer interval.
Device I/O deltas were zero during every hold. A read-write to read-only
update returned EOPNOTSUPP; subsequent writes and normal unmount succeeded.
The combined module passed fsxlite (10000 operations), 400 concurrent fsyncs,
and online plus offline checks. Laptop suspend/resume was not tested.
